ABSTRACT
This paper presents a practical, phased pathway from the ST-T1024 software reference implementation toward a physical silicon implementation.
Rather than proposing a costly, high-risk “big bang” tapeout, the pathway begins with simulation and FPGA prototyping, proceeds through hardware validation, and can ultimately culminate in a custom ASIC.
The central architectural proposition is simplicity. ST-T1024 is designed around deterministic execution, ternary computation, bounded state transitions, domain-specific tiles, local memory, and aggressive power management. The architecture therefore provides a potentially attractive basis for hardware implementation without depending on the large floating-point compute and memory infrastructure characteristic of conventional cloud-scale AI accelerators.
The critical strategic point is that ST-T1024 is not proposed as a single centralized product-development program. It is an open standard and a self-service engineering platform.
A start-up, independent engineer, semiconductor company, large technology organization, or sovereign institution can independently study the public repository, execute the reference implementation, reproduce the documented behavior, develop its own RTL, verify the design, and pursue an appropriate hardware implementation.
The repository contains the knowledge. The standard is open. The path is clear.
No permission is required. No centralized implementation is required. No spoon-feeding is required.


1. INTRODUCTION: WHY Si NOW?
ST-T1024 currently exists as a software reference implementation and architectural specification.
The reference implementation provides a concrete environment in which the deterministic execution model, domain triage, finite-state orchestration, tile concept, ternary computation, and power-down behavior can be studied and reproduced.
Software execution, however, is not the final deployment target.
The natural engineering progression is:
Reference implementation → Simulation → FPGA → Hardware validation → RTL refinement → ASIC
