3. DETERMINISM AND SIMULATION
Determinism is particularly important for the silicon pathway.
A deterministic architecture provides a defined relationship between:
Input + defined state + defined configuration
and
Output + execution path + execution cost.
This makes simulation and verification central components of the development process.
The proposed progression is:
Stage 1 — Reference simulation
Execute the ST-T1024 reference implementation and establish reproducible behavior.
Stage 2 — Cycle-oriented model
Translate the architectural operations into an explicit cycle model and verify execution bounds.
Stage 3 — RTL simulation
Implement the hardware architecture in RTL and compare RTL behavior against the reference model.
Stage 4 — FPGA
Run the RTL implementation on real hardware.
Stage 5 — ASIC
After verification and hardware measurements, proceed to physical implementation if the results justify it.
This provides an important advantage:
The path to silicon does not begin with silicon.
A substantial portion of the architecture can be studied, simulated, tested, and corrected before fabrication.


